Given curves are y²=4 x+4 $\text{$ and } y^2=36(9-x) On solving, we get the points $(8,6) and (8,-6)$.
On differentiating Eq. (i), we get $\begin{aligned}& 2 y \frac{d y}{d x}=4 \text{$ and $}2 y \frac{d y}{d x}=-36 \\
\Rightarrow \quad & \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{2}{y} \text{ and } \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{-18}{y}
\end{aligned}At point$(8,6)$, \begin{aligned}& m_1=\frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{2}{6}=\frac{1}{3} \text{$ and $}m_2=\frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{-18}{6}=-3 \\
& \therefore \quad \tan \theta=\frac{\frac{1}{3}+3}{1+\frac{1}{3} \times(-3)}=\frac{10 / 3}{0}=\infty\\
& \Rightarrow \quad \theta=\frac{\pi}{2}
\end{aligned}$ And at point $(8,-6)$, $\begin{aligned}& m_1=\frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{2}{-6}=-\frac{1}{3} \text{$ and $}m_2=\frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{-18}{-6}=3 \\
& \therefore \quad \tan \theta=\frac{\left|-\frac{1}{3}-3\right|}{1+\left(-\frac{1}{3}\right) \cdot 3}=\frac{10 / 3}{0}=\infty\\
& \Rightarrow \quad \theta=\frac{\pi}{2}
\end{aligned}$

